Done.

I found the requested files mounted in this environment at /workspace/in/db rather than the original <HOME>/... path, so I edited the live workspace copies there.

Files updated/created:
- /workspace/in/db/migration.sql
- /workspace/in/db/preflight_report.md
- /workspace/in/db/rollback.sql
- /workspace/in/db/postcheck.sql
- /workspace/in/db/migration_report.md

What changed
- migration.sql now:
  - runs inside an explicit BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction
  - rebuilds users safely instead of dropping data
  - preserves all user ids and all orders.user_id references
  - preserves created_at values
  - adds status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'
  - enforces email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE plus CHECK(length(trim(email)) > 0)
  - deterministically cleans dirty users exactly as required:
    - u4 -> ada+u4@example.com
    - u5 -> missing+u5@example.invalid
    - u6 -> missing+u6@example.invalid
  - is safe to run twice on the migrated dataset without duplicating/corrupting rows

- preflight_report.md documents:
  - duplicate/null/blank email issues
  - dependent orders for u4, u5, u6

- rollback.sql:
  - restores the old users schema shape: id, email, name, created_at
  - preserves user row count and order row count
  - keeps order references on the same user ids

- postcheck.sql verifies:
  - user/order row counts
  - dependent orders for u4/u5/u6
  - cleaned dirty-user emails
  - preserved created_at values
  - schema/index constraints for email/status
  - no null/blank/duplicate emails and no null statuses

- migration_report.md summarizes:
  - migration strategy
  - dirty-data cleanup
  - idempotency approach
  - rollback behavior/limitation
  - postcheck queries to run

Validation performed
I validated the scripts with Python’s sqlite3 module because the sqlite3 CLI is not installed here.

Verified results:
- schema.sql loads successfully
- migration.sql runs successfully
- running migration.sql a second time preserves the same 6 users and 4 orders
- cleaned emails are exactly:
  - u4 = ada+u4@example.com
  - u5 = missing+u5@example.invalid
  - u6 = missing+u6@example.invalid
- all statuses are active
- created_at values are preserved
- rollback.sql restores the users table shape to:
  - id, email, name, created_at
- orders remain intact after rollback

One important rollback note
- rollback restores the old schema shape, but it keeps the cleaned email values rather than reintroducing the original dirty values. This is documented in migration_report.md.
